  "query_preview": "..\nA weak disturbance dubbed Invest 95W (discussed in the Tropical \nSystems section below) by the Joint Typhoon Warning Center (JTWC) \nwill bring scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms tonight\nthrough Saturday night across the region. Conditions will remain\nbelow hazardous criteria, so no Watches, Warnings, and Advisories \n(WWAs) were issued at this time. 95W will pass between Guam and Rota\nover the next day, so conditions are expected to quickly improve on \nSunday. The heaviest convection and showers are found along its \nsouthern peripheries, so the zones were split between Guam and the \nCNMI to account for a higher concentration of showers over the \nsouthern tip of the island chain. Gentle to moderate winds are \nexpected through the weekend with a counterclockwise change in \ndirection through Saturday, eventually becoming gentle \nnorthwesterlies early next week. Then, a dry spell will ensue across \nthe region, with the possibility of isolated showers and light and \nvariable winds during the former half of next week. Active weather \nmay return late next week as models hint at a potential tropical \ndisturbance approaching the islands from the east. Since the \ntimeframe is beyond the next five days, uncertainty is high, but we \nwill continue monitoring this situation. As a reminder, October is \nwithin peak typhoon season in the western Pacific, so residents of \nthe Marianas should monitor the forecast over the next several days \nand maintain a general state of preparedness.",
